Employee productivity in the public sector has become a critical concern for policymakers and administrators, particularly in the context of increasing service demands, limited resources, and ongoing organizational reforms. Among the factors influencing productivity, management value systems play a vital role in shaping managerial behaviour, decision-making, and employee attitudes. This study examines the relationship between value systems and employee productivity among public sector employees, with specific emphasis on value orientations such as conformist, sociocentric, existential, tribalistic, egocentric, and manipulative values.&nbsp;
<div>
 The research addresses the problem of inconsistent productivity levels in public sector organizations despite structural and policy interventions, raising the question of whether underlying value system contribute to this issue. Accordingly, the main research question investigates how different value system dimensions influence employee productivity in the public sector. A quantitative research design was adopted, and data were collected using a structured questionnaire administered to public sector employees. Descriptive statistics, Pearson correlation analysis, and multiple regression analysis were employed to analyse the data.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings reveal a moderate and statistically significant positive relationship between overall value system and employee productivity. Regression results indicate that value system collectively explain a meaningful proportion of variance in productivity. At the dimensional level, manipulative value orientation shows a significant positive effect on employee productivity, while egocentric values exhibit a significant negative influence. Other value dimensions, including conformist, sociocentric, existential, and tribalistic values, do not demonstrate statistically significant effects when considered simultaneously.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The study concludes that value system are a key determinant of employee productivity in the public sector, though their impact varies by value orientation. The findings underscore the importance of promoting value-based management practices that encourage strategic influence and organizational focus while minimizing self-centred managerial tendencies. The study contributes to the literature on public sector management by highlighting the behavioural foundations of productivity and offers practical insights for improving managerial effectiveness and organizational performance.&nbsp;
</div>

How did development actually happen? How can poor societies escape the chicken-and-egg problem of development: growth or good governance first?&nbsp;
<div>
 &nbsp;Yuen Yuen Ang challenges linear, Western-centric theories of development—the IndustrialColonial Paradigm—which are trapped in the false debate over whether growth or good governance should come first and assume that “good governance” must take forms that exist in the West.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Using China’s capitalist revolution from the 1980s to 2012 as her primary demonstration site, Ang advances a paradigm shift toward a systems-based, pluralistic, and reflexive political economy—what she now formalizes as AIM (Adaptive, Inclusive, Moral).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 &nbsp;Applying AIM, Ang shows that China’s transformation was not driven by centralized control but by Directed Improvisation—direction from Beijing paired with bottom-up improvisation among local governments. This system of adaptive governance enabled a coevolutionary process of development: local actors began by harnessing normatively weak institutions to build markets— “using what they have”—before later establishing modern institutions to 2 preserve markets.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 As a paradigm-building book, How China Escaped the Poverty Trap introduced a family of interconnected concepts, theories, and methods: Complex vs. Complicated; Influence vs. Control; Institutions vs. Meta-institutions; Directed Improvisation; Coevolutionary Development; Mapping Coevolution; Market-Building vs. M-Preserving; Speed Money vs. Access Money, and more. Together, these ideas form an intellectual forest that has continued to generate new analyses and applications, including Ang’s second book, China’s Gilded Age (2020).&nbsp;
</div>
